        "action": "Remove or constrain the policy statement granting read actions to Principal \"*\" or AWS: \"*\". If broad read access is genuinely required, add a restricting Condition on aws:PrincipalOrgID, aws:SourceVpc, or a fixed aws:SourceIp CIDR. Enable S3 Public Access Block with BlockPublicPolicy set to true to reject future policies that grant public read."
      },
      "classification": "aggregate_check",
      "scope_tags": [
        "aws",
        "s3"
      ],
      "defect": "The bucket policy contains a Statement with Effect \"Allow\", Principal \"*\" (or AWS: \"*\"), and a read action such as s3:GetObject or s3:GetObjectAcl. No restricting Condition narrows the principal to a specific account, VPC, IP range, or organization.\n",
      "infection": "Any unauthenticated HTTP caller can fetch objects from this bucket. Distinct from ACL-driven exposure in that policy- based public read often applies to specific prefixes or objects, not the whole bucket — but adopters miss the distinction and treat \"policy allows public read\" as \"this bucket is public.\" Search engines and scanners enumerate buckets via GetBucketPolicy lookups specifically to find policy-granted read access, because it persists even when ACLs are tightened.\n",
      "failure": "Data exposure for objects matched by the policy's Resource clause. Scope depends on whether the policy grants read on the whole bucket (arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*) or a subset — but adopters commonly over-scope the Resource clause during authoring, so \"intentional limited public read\" often becomes \"entire bucket public\" in practice. Audit the policy's Resource ARNs to understand exposure surface.\n",
